    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A curved treadmill has no motor and no speed buttons. You run on a slatted belt that sits
          in a shallow concave curve, and where you place your feet on that curve sets the pace:
          move forward and it speeds up, drift back and it slows. The AssaultRunner Pro is the
          best-known home version of that idea, and its core figures are consistent across
          Assault's listing and independent coverage. Its warranty is not, and its stock has been
          patchy. This page covers both, along with the more important question of whether a
          curved trainer suits the way you actually train.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. AssaultRunner Pro Treadmill: The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>The AssaultRunner Pro treadmill is a well-made, motorless curved trainer for people who
            want to run hard indoors — sprints, intervals, conditioning work — and it is a poor fit for
            walking, for steady easy miles, or for anyone who needs to fold a machine away.</strong> At
            about $3,499 it costs as much as a premium motorised treadmill with incline, so the question is
            less "is it good?" than "is a curved trainer the right tool for you?"
          </p>
          <p>
            Its core figures are consistent wherever we looked: a 17 by 62-inch slat running surface, a
            350 lb user rating, a 280 lb frame, no motor and no need for a power outlet. Two things are
            less settled. The frame warranty is 7 years on Assault's current page but 5 years on a US
            dealer listing and in independent reviews, and Assault's own store showed the Pro sold out on
            the day we checked, while a dealer listed it as available. Get the warranty term in writing
            from whoever sells it to you, and check stock before planning around a delivery date.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you are deciding between curved trainers, our
            <a href="/best-curved-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best curved treadmills</a>
            roundup puts the Pro next to the SOLE C80, TrueForm and Woodway machines. If you are deciding
            whether you want a manual treadmill at all, start with
            <a href="/how-to-use-a-manual-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to use a manual treadmill</a>.
            We have not tested the Pro; this review is built from the published figures and from what
            independent reviewers consistently report.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

