      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <div id="how-it-works" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. How a Curved, Motorless Treadmill Works</h2>
<p>
            The running surface on the Pro is not a belt over a flat board. It is a row of slats, each
            topped with a TPU surface, running on bearings around a frame shaped into a shallow concave
            curve. There is nothing driving it. When you stand at the bottom of the curve, the belt stays
            still. Step forward onto the rising front of the curve and gravity, plus the push of your
            stride, rolls the slats backwards; the further forward you run, the faster the belt goes. Ease
            back towards the bottom and it slows.
          </p>
          <p>
            That design has three consequences that decide whether the machine suits you.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">There is no speed limit, and no speed setting</h3>
          <p>
            A motorised treadmill tops out at 10, 12 or 14 mph. The Pro goes as fast as you can run.
            That is the reason sprinters, field-sport athletes and conditioning coaches like curved
            trainers: maximal efforts are possible indoors, and the belt never pulls you along at a pace
            you set before you tired. The flip side is that you cannot dial in a pace and settle into it.
            Holding a steady 9:00 mile means holding your position on the curve by feel, which takes
            attention, especially late in a run.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">It is harder work than a motorised belt</h3>
          <p>
            You are driving the belt yourself, so effort at a given pace is higher than on a motorised
            treadmill. One independent review likens it to running uphill and advises expecting to run
            slower than you would outdoors or on a powered machine; another says the curve does not feel
            quite like a natural running surface. For conditioning,
            that is the point. For a runner whose plan calls for easy aerobic miles at a specific pace, it
            is a complication.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">It needs no power</h3>
          <p>
            The drive needs no outlet; the console runs on four AA batteries. That makes a garage, a
            basement corner or an outbuilding without a dedicated circuit workable, and it removes the
            drive motor and motor controller, which are where many motorised treadmills eventually fail.
            Our <a href="/manual-vs-motorized-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">manual vs motorised treadmill</a>
            comparison sets out the full trade-off, and the
            <a href="/curved-treadmill-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">curved treadmill guide</a>
            explains the mechanics in more depth.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="specifications" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. The Specifications, and Where Sources Disagree</h2>
<p>
            The upper rows below are figures that Assault publishes and at least one independent source
            repeats. The lower rows are where the sources do not agree, or where only Assault gives a
            figure. Everything was read on 14 October 2026.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Specification</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Figure</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Status</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Running surface</td>
                <td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">17 x 62 in., curved TPU slats</td>
                <td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Assault and independent reviews agree</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Maximum user weight</td>
                <td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">350 lb</td>
                <td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Assault, dealer and reviews agree</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Assembled weight</td>
                <td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">280 lb</td>
                <td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Assault, dealer and one review agree; one review's text says 290 lb</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Power</td>
                <td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">No motor; console on 4 AA batteries</td>
                <td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Assault and reviews agree</td>
