          <p>
            None of the disagreements change what the machine is. The warranty one matters because it is
            money: two extra years of frame cover, if they apply to your unit, are worth having in writing.
            The dimension one matters only if the space you have is within an inch or two of the machine;
            in that case measure for the largest figure.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="deck-and-running" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. The Running Surface: Width, Feel and the Learning Curve</h2>
<p>
            Seventeen inches is narrow by treadmill standards. A typical motorised home treadmill is 20
            inches wide and a running-focused one 22. Independent reviewers of the Pro say the width
            requires you to stay centred, and both reviews we read flag it as something that takes getting
            used to. Among curved trainers it is not unusual — the Sunny SF-X7110 in our curved roundup is
            similar — but the SOLE C80's 19-inch surface gives noticeably more lateral margin. For a broad
            runner, or anyone whose stride wanders when tired, that is worth weighing.
          </p>
          <p>
            Sixty-two inches of length is generous. The curve means not all of it is usable at every pace,
            but for a full sprinting stride, length is not the constraint on this machine; width is.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What the slats feel like</h3>
          <p>
            Slats give a firm, slightly springy footing that is different from a cushioned motorised deck.
            Assault uses a TPU surface on the Pro's slats. There is no published standard that lets you
            compare the softness of one brand's slats with another's, so if feel matters to you, try one in
            a gym before buying if a local gym or performance facility has a curved trainer on the floor.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The learning curve</h3>
          <p>
            Both independent reviews we read describe a learning curve, and they agree on its shape. The
            first sessions feel unnatural: the belt responds to small shifts in position, and it can run
            away from you if you lean forward. Starting conservatively and holding the handrails while you
            find the neutral point helps, and one reviewer says the form comes within a couple of runs.
            Getting off at speed takes care too, because the belt does not stop on command; you slow it by
            moving back down the curve. Our
            <a href="/how-to-use-a-manual-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to using a manual treadmill</a>
            covers how to start, how to stop and how to hold a pace, and
            <a href="/treadmill-running-form/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill running form</a>
            covers the posture points that matter more on a curve than on a flat belt.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Walking on it</h3>
          <p>
            Reviewers disagree here. One found walking smooth and easy to hold; another said the narrow
            surface and heavy-duty design make it less suited to walking and overkill for anyone who mainly
            walks. Both points can be true at once: you can walk on the Pro comfortably, but if walking is
            what you will mostly do, you are paying for a sprint machine to do a job a cheaper flat walker
            or a motorised treadmill does as well.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="console" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. The Console and Connectivity</h2>
<p>
            The Pro's console runs on four AA batteries and, on Assault's description, shows time,
            calories, speed, RPM and heart rate; independent reviews also list distance. It connects over
            Bluetooth and ANT+, which means it can pair with a chest-strap heart-rate monitor, and Assault's
            page describes the screen on the Pro as larger and higher-contrast than the Classic's.
          </p>
          <p>
            The onboard programmes are simple: Assault lists interval, target, quick-start and competition
            modes. Independent reviewers
            describe the console as straightforward and the onboard workouts as limited, with Assault's own
            app adding more. That is the right expectation. This is not a screen-led machine and there is no
            subscription required to use it; you bring the plan.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two small practical points come up in both reviews we read: there is no water-bottle holder on
            the Pro (Assault's comparison table confirms the Elite has two and the Pro none), and the phone
            ledge is basic. If you train by heart rate, our guide to
            <a href="/best-heart-rate-monitors-for-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">heart-rate monitors for treadmill use</a>
            covers which straps pair over Bluetooth and ANT+, and
            <a href="/chest-strap-vs-wrist-heart-rate/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">chest strap vs wrist</a>
            explains why a strap reads more reliably during sprints.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="workouts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. What It Is Best For: Sprints, Intervals and Conditioning</h2>
<p>
            The Pro's strengths line up with a specific kind of training. Because the belt has no speed
            ceiling and responds immediately to effort, it suits maximal sprints, short intervals with fast
            transitions, and conditioning circuits where you step on and off between other stations. There
            is no wait for a motor to spin up or slow down; you start running and the belt goes.
          </p>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/curved-treadmill-workouts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">curved treadmill workouts</a>
            page sets out sessions written for exactly this kind of machine — sprint repeats, tempo blocks
            held by feel, and mixed conditioning. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-sprint-workout/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill sprint workout</a>
            and <a href="/treadmill-hiit-workout/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">HIIT workout</a>
            guides cover the principles that apply on any treadmill.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What it is less good for</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Pace-specific easy running.</strong> You can hold a steady pace by feel, but you cannot set one. For a training plan built on exact paces, a motorised treadmill is easier.</li>
            <li><strong>Incline work.</strong> There is no incline. Running further up the curve is harder, but it is not an adjustable grade.</li>
            <li><strong>Sled-style pushes.</strong> The Pro has no resistance setting. Curved trainers with magnetic resistance, such as the SOLE C80, can be loaded for pushes against the handrails.</li>
            <li><strong>Long, mostly walking sessions.</strong> As above, it works, but it is an expensive way to walk.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            A note on heart rate and calories: because you are driving the belt, your heart rate at a given
            speed will typically be higher than on a motorised treadmill. Train to heart rate or perceived
            effort rather than to the speed readout when you move between machines.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty-and-stock" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Warranty, Price and Stock</h2>
<p>
            Assault's current page for the Pro lists a 7-year frame warranty, 3 years on moving parts and a
            150,000-mile belt, with labour marked as not applicable in its comparison table. A US dealer
            listing on the same day, and two independent reviews from 2024, give 5 years on the frame with
            the same parts and belt terms. Our curved and manual roundups recorded the same conflict a day
            earlier. The likeliest explanation is that Assault extended the frame term and older listings
            have not caught up, but that is our inference; we cannot confirm which term applies to a unit
            bought from a dealer. Ask the seller, quote the frame term in writing, and register the machine
            with Assault.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What the warranty is worth here</h3>
          <p>
            On a motorless machine the warranty is shaped differently from a motorised treadmill's. There
            is no motor or motor controller to cover, which removes the most expensive single failure. What
            wears are the slats, the bearings and the belt assembly, which is why the 150,000-mile belt
            term and the 3-year moving-parts term matter more than the frame figure in day-to-day ownership.
            A frame failure on a 280 lb steel machine used within its 350 lb rating is unusual either way.
            Our <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a>
            explains how to read these terms and what registration requirements to watch for.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Price</h3>
          <p>
            On 14 October 2026 Assault listed the Pro at $3,499, and a US dealer listed it at the same price.
            A 2024 independent review quoted a lower $2,999 list, so the price appears to have risen. Assault's comparison puts the
            Elite at $4,499. Shipping, delivery and assembly charges vary by seller, so compare delivered
            prices.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Stock</h3>
          <p>
            Assault's own store showed the Pro sold out on the day we checked, as it did on 13 October when
            we wrote our roundups. The US dealer listing showed it as available, though without a stock
            count. If you are planning around a date, ask the dealer for a confirmed delivery window before
            paying, and be wary of listings that are much cheaper than $3,499 without an explanation.
            Some retailers outside the US still list the machine under the older Assault AirRunner name.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="space-and-delivery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Space, Delivery and Moving It</h2>
<p>
            The Pro is about 70 inches long, 32 to 33 inches wide and 64 inches tall, depending on the
            source, and it does not fold. Treat it as a permanent fixture and plan the space with clearance
            behind the deck and at the sides for stepping on and off. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">dimensions and space guide</a>
            covers clearance figures, and
            <a href="/treadmill-in-garage/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">using a treadmill in a garage</a>
            covers temperature, damp and floors, which matter because a motorless machine is often bought
            for exactly that kind of space.
          </p>
          <p>
            At 280 lb assembled it is heavy. It has two front transport wheels and a rear handle, and one
            reviewer could move it alone on a hard floor, though not as a routine. On carpet or over a
            threshold it is a two-person job. The boxed machine is heavier again — Assault gives two
            different figures, 359.8 and 373.7 lb — so plan the delivery route, and do not plan on carrying
            it up stairs without help. Both reviews recommend a second person for unboxing and assembly.
          </p>
          <p>
            Because it needs no outlet, location is limited only by floor strength and space. A stable,
            level floor matters more than usual on a curved trainer, because a machine that rocks will feel
            unsettled at sprint pace; a firm equipment mat helps on hard floors and protects them.
          </p>
        </div>

