        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Alternatives Worth Comparing</h2>
<p>
            The Pro's nearest rivals, all covered with verified figures in our
            <a href="/best-curved-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">curved treadmill roundup</a>
            and our <a href="/best-manual-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">manual treadmill roundup</a>,
            differ from it in ways that matter more than their badges.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>SOLE C80.</strong> A curved slat trainer with a wider 19 by 62-inch surface and eight levels of magnetic resistance, rated to 330 lb and usually priced well below the Pro. The wider deck and the resistance are its two advantages; the Pro's longer track record and higher user rating are its own.</li>
            <li><strong>TrueForm Trainer.</strong> A shallower curve — TrueForm says the shallowest on the market, and independent reviews describe it as shallower than rivals — with a 400 lb rating, at a similar or slightly higher price. Worth a look for runners who care about a natural stride more than sprint response.</li>
            <li><strong>Woodway Curve LTG.</strong> Woodway's home-oriented curved trainer, rated to 350 lb, with a TPE slat belt Woodway describes as softer than average. It costs about the same or a little more, when you can get one.</li>
            <li><strong>A motorised treadmill with incline.</strong> For the same $3,500, or much less, a motorised machine gives you pace control, incline and often folding. If your training is mostly steady running and walking, that is the better buy. Our <a href="/manual-vs-motorized-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">manual vs motorised comparison</a> sets out where each wins.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Do not compare the Pro with $300 to $500 machines sold as "curved" folding treadmills; they are a
            different product, and our roundups explain why.
          </p>
          <p>
            A note on safety records: we searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission's recall listings
            on 14 October 2026 and found no recall or safety warning involving Assault Fitness or the
            AssaultRunner.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Who Should Buy the AssaultRunner Pro</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Buy it if you want to run hard indoors.</strong> Sprinters, field-sport and combat-sport
            athletes, and anyone whose training is built on intervals and conditioning will get the most from
            a belt with no speed ceiling that responds immediately to effort.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if you want a machine with no motor to fail and no outlet to find.</strong> For a
            garage gym or an outbuilding, a well-built motorless trainer is a long-lived piece of equipment,
            and the 150,000-mile belt term reflects that.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if you are within its 350 lb rating and have a permanent place for it.</strong> It
            does not fold, and at 280 lb it stays where it is put.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Look at the SOLE C80 if you want resistance or a wider surface</strong>, and at the
            TrueForm Trainer if a natural, shallow-curve stride matters more than sprint response.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not buy it to walk, or to run easy miles at a set pace.</strong> A motorised treadmill
            does those jobs better for the same money or less, with incline and pace control included.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Whatever you decide, confirm two things before paying:</strong> that the seller has stock
            and a delivery date, and which frame warranty term applies to your unit, in writing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the AssaultRunner Pro treadmill worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is worth it for people who will use it for hard running — sprints, intervals and conditioning — and have a permanent place for a 280 lb machine. At about $3,499 it is poor value for walking or steady easy running, which a motorised treadmill does better for less. We have not tested it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the weight limit on the AssaultRunner Pro?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Assault rates the Pro for users up to 350 lb, and a US dealer listing and independent reviews give the same figure. Assault lists the AssaultRunner Elite at 400 lb if you need more headroom.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the warranty on the AssaultRunner Pro?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Assault’s current page lists 7 years on the frame, 3 years on moving parts and a 150,000-mile belt. A US dealer listing and two independent reviews give 5 years on the frame with the same parts and belt terms. Ask the seller which term applies to your unit and get it in writing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the AssaultRunner Pro need electricity?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. The belt is driven entirely by your running, so it needs no outlet. The console runs on four AA batteries.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">AssaultRunner Pro vs Elite: what is the difference?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On Assault’s listing the Elite adds a 400 lb rating against 350 lb, a 10-year frame warranty with one year of labour and a lifetime belt, a backlit console and two bottle holders, for $4,499 against $3,499. For a home runner within the Pro’s rating, the running experience is the same.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you walk on an AssaultRunner Pro?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, by staying near the bottom of the curve. Independent reviewers disagree on how comfortable it is: one found walking smooth, another found the 17-inch surface and heavy design ill-suited to it. If you will mostly walk, a motorised treadmill or a flat manual walker is the better use of the money.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying an AssaultRunner Pro</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most disappointed buyers of curved trainers bought the right machine for someone else’s training. These are the avoidable errors.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying it mainly to walk</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The Pro can be walked on, but it is a sprint and conditioning machine with a 17-inch surface. For mostly walking, a motorised treadmill with incline or a flat manual walker does the job as well for much less money.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting motorised-treadmill paces</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">You drive the belt yourself, so the same speed takes more effort; one independent review likens it to running uphill. Train to heart rate or effort when you move between machines, rather than chasing the pace you run on a powered belt.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not confirming the frame warranty</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Assault’s current page says 7 years on the frame; a dealer listing and independent reviews say 5. Two years of frame cover is worth a short email. Ask the seller which term applies and keep the answer with your order.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Planning around a delivery date without confirmed stock</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Assault’s store showed the Pro sold out on 13 and 14 October 2026, while a dealer listed it as available. Ask for a confirmed delivery window before paying, and be wary of prices well below $3,499 without a clear reason.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

